  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nOur primary approach refines the search space by treating the step function as a continuous representation, circumventing discretization-related limitations. The convex-hull of the [0,1] hypercube allows gradient-based optimization that minimizes autocorrelation terms through an analytical objective function. We target the maximum overlap integral $(C_5)$ with principled reduction achieved by gradient descent on the continuous distribution.\n\nMathematical Specification\nWe represent the step function $h: [0,2] \\to [0,1]$ by samples $h_i$, with $\\sum h_i \\cdot dx = 1$ ($dx=2/N$). The objective to minimize is:\n\\[\n\\max_k\\left( \\frac{1}{dx} \\cdot \\sum_{i}\\int_{x_i}^{x_{i}+dx} h(x) \\cdot (1 - h(x+k))  dx \\right)\n\\]\nconverted into discrete form using `np.correlate(h, 1.0 - h, mode='full')`. We derive gradients component-wise for efficient Adam optimization. Enforcement of $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$ and $\\sum h_i dx = 1$ is achieved via post-update projection. We use Lagrange dual variables to relate the integral constraint.\n\nImplementation Procedure\nfunction run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs) -> Tuple[np.ndarray, float, int]:\n    Initialize parent and configuration\n    while time budget remains:\n        Stage 0: Projection and normalization\n        Stage 1: Gradient ascent with Adam (function h)\n        Stage 2: Gradient descent for overlap reduction\n        Stage 3: Parallel optimization with different constraints\n        Stage 4: Dimension-aware candidate evaluation\n        Stage 5: Gradient-based polishing with projected descent\n    Return best solution\n\nSearch Choices\n- Initial symmetry testing: parent, alt, random patterns\n- Default N: 380, other N optimized via regression\n- Adam: tailored learning rate and q-leaning factor\n- Projection adjustment: stochastic rounding\n\nBudget Estimate\n> 80% time for gradient computation and projection\n\nValidation\n(1) Check integral = sum(h)*dx == 1\n(2) Verify all h in [0,1]\n(3) Evaluate objective via correlation only on feasible points\n\nTheoretical Recap:\nDerivatives are taken for the function at the extreme lag. The combinatorial optimization phase ensures maximum overlap reduction under continuous constraints. By operating continuously, we lower the variance in constructed functions relative to discrete samples that may violate integrality constraints.",
